INT. FREIGHT ELEVATOR, MUSEUM OF CLOCKS - NIGHT

Cage steel. Brass fittings. Descending.

VERA KOLOSOVA (40s, black coveralls) and PETRA LINDGREN-HAYES (40s, black coveralls) share a tool case. Between them, in a silk-lined crate, ticks a golden carriage clock. BARRY "BOLT" UCHENNA (30s, headset, earpiece) crouches with a laptop.

VERA
Hold it level.

PETRA
I am holding it level.

VERA
She is not holding it level.

BOLT
Hey. Hey. She's right. Ladies, please.

PETRA
Which she?

BOLT
Either one.

A floor counter flickers. B2. B3.

VERA
(whispering)
Guard's rounds at three-fifteen. We have forty seconds.

PETRA
Thirty-eight now.

BOLT
Thirty-seven.

The elevator shudders. Stops. Lights die.

Silence. A single tick from the clock.

VERA
Don't breathe.

PETRA
^Don't breathe.

BOLT
I never breathe, ladies. It's a professional habit.

Footsteps overhead. Slow. A flashlight beam slides down the shaft. The three freeze. The clock ticks. Louder. Louder.

PETRA
Cover it.

Vera drapes her jacket over the crate. The ticking muffles. The beam drifts, hesitates, fades. Footsteps walk away.

Lights hum back to life. The floor counter reads B4.

BOLT
Forty seconds.

VERA
Twenty-two.

PETRA
Fifteen.

The doors open on a loading dock. Moonlight. A van.
